1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,887
By the end of this video, you will know how to skate into a headwind, through a crosswind, and with a tailwind.

2
00:00:06,887 --> 00:00:12,472
You will also know how to plan a windy day route so the wind helps you when you are tired.

3
00:00:12,472 --> 00:00:17,250
Wind is the road condition skaters complain about most and prepare for least.

4
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:20,601
A steady headwind quietly flattens your average speed.

5
00:00:20,601 --> 00:00:24,013
It can make you wonder whether your bearings are dying.

6
00:00:24,013 --> 00:00:25,378
They probably are not.

7
00:00:25,378 --> 00:00:29,846
Here is what the wind is really doing, and what you can change about it.

8
00:00:30,546 --> 00:00:35,499
Air resistance climbs with the square of the speed you move through the air.

9
00:00:35,499 --> 00:00:39,604
Double the airspeed and the drag is roughly four times greater.

10
00:00:39,604 --> 00:00:44,101
That is why a headwind punishes fast skaters far more than slow ones.

11
00:00:44,101 --> 00:00:45,274
Now the key point.

12
00:00:45,274 --> 00:00:48,011
Your watch measures speed over the ground.

13
00:00:48,011 --> 00:00:51,400
The air only cares about your speed through the air.

14
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:59,089
Skate at 25 kilometres per hour into a 15 kilometres per hour wind, and the air pushes back as hard as it would at 40.

15
00:00:59,089 --> 00:01:02,478
But you only get 25 of ground speed for that effort.

16
00:01:02,478 --> 00:01:05,476
You are doing expensive work at a cheap speed.

17
00:01:05,476 --> 00:01:08,604
That mismatch explains the two classic mistakes.

18
00:01:08,604 --> 00:01:14,403
Skaters either chase their usual average until they blow up, or they abandon the session.

19
00:01:15,103 --> 00:01:17,069
This diagram shows two skaters.

20
00:01:17,069 --> 00:01:22,140
The one on the left has plenty of knee bend, but the chest still faces the wind.

21
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:28,987
The one on the right has dropped the chest toward the thigh, so the torso presents far less area to the air.

22
00:01:28,987 --> 00:01:33,741
That is the difference between bending your knees and actually getting low.

23
00:01:33,741 --> 00:01:37,672
The drawing is schematic and not to scale, but the idea holds.

24
00:01:37,672 --> 00:01:44,962
Frontal area is the biggest variable you control, and you control it with your hips and your back, not your wallet.

25
00:01:45,662 --> 00:01:48,771
Before you think about equipment, fix your shape.

26
00:01:48,771 --> 00:01:51,118
Drop your chest closer to your thigh.

27
00:01:51,118 --> 00:01:56,764
Flatten your back, because a hunched upper back makes a shelf the wind stacks up against.

28
00:01:56,764 --> 00:02:02,600
Tuck your head so your helmet sits in line with your back, with your eyes still up the road.

29
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:09,007
Carry one arm on the back, since two wide swinging arms add width exactly where you cannot afford it.

30
00:02:09,007 --> 00:02:14,399
And keep the recovery tight, so a trailing leg does not give the air another surface.

31
00:02:14,399 --> 00:02:22,836
If this low shape makes your lower back complain within minutes, it is often a mobility and strength limit, and off skate work helps.

32
00:02:22,836 --> 00:02:32,478
But if the pain is sharp, spreads into a leg, comes with numbness, or lasts several days, see a doctor or a physiotherapist before you train through it.

33
00:02:33,178 --> 00:02:36,898
The instinct in a headwind is to push harder and longer.

34
00:02:36,898 --> 00:02:38,691
That is the wrong instinct.

35
00:02:38,691 --> 00:02:46,329
A long, slow, heavily loaded push is where skaters stall, because your leg runs out of force before the glide ends.

36
00:02:46,329 --> 00:02:49,650
Instead, shorten the stroke and raise the cadence.

37
00:02:49,650 --> 00:02:54,698
More pushes per minute, each slightly less loaded, so the leg keeps cycling.

38
00:02:54,698 --> 00:02:57,620
Think of it as dropping into an easier gear.

39
00:02:57,620 --> 00:03:00,941
The wind steals your glide, so stop paying for it.

40
00:03:00,941 --> 00:03:03,731
Set the skate down, push, recover, repeat.

41
00:03:03,731 --> 00:03:09,842
Keep the push sideways, because a push that drifts backwards wastes effort you cannot spare.

42
00:03:09,842 --> 00:03:12,166
And do not stand up for a breather.

43
00:03:12,166 --> 00:03:16,019
Standing tall into a headwind is like opening a parachute.

44
00:03:16,019 --> 00:03:19,008
If you need to recover, recover low and soft.

45
00:03:19,707 --> 00:03:21,433
A headwind makes you slow.

46
00:03:21,433 --> 00:03:23,889
A crosswind can put you on the floor.

47
00:03:23,889 --> 00:03:26,213
This diagram shows why gaps matter.

48
00:03:26,213 --> 00:03:33,714
Wind speeds up as it squeezes through a gap between buildings, past the end of a wall, or at a bridge or flyover.

49
00:03:33,714 --> 00:03:38,227
So the shove lands right at the gap, where you have the least space.

50
00:03:38,227 --> 00:03:42,343
On the approach, ease the effort and leave room on both sides.

51
00:03:42,343 --> 00:03:44,334
In any crosswind, stay boring.

52
00:03:44,334 --> 00:03:50,972
Get low so the wind has less leverage, keep your weight centred, and cut out long one footed glides.

53
00:03:50,972 --> 00:04:00,133
Leave a wider buffer from the kerb and traffic, and keep a hand free to balance, because on a gusty day the wind picks your line, not you.

54
00:04:00,833 --> 00:04:04,062
Wind behind you is lovely and slightly dishonest.

55
00:04:04,062 --> 00:04:10,058
Your average jumps and your legs feel strong, but your fitness did not change this morning.

56
00:04:10,058 --> 00:04:13,748
So a tailwind personal best is not evidence of progress.

57
00:04:13,748 --> 00:04:19,481
Test yourself on a calm day, or over an out and back route that covers both directions.

58
00:04:19,481 --> 00:04:22,249
Better still, use the tailwind on purpose.

59
00:04:22,249 --> 00:04:28,113
It is the one time the road lets you hold a very high leg speed without drag capping you.

60
00:04:28,113 --> 00:04:32,133
That makes it the best place on your loop for overspeed work.

61
00:04:32,133 --> 00:04:39,777
Do short efforts at a cadence above anything you could hold into the wind, and teach your legs to turn over quickly.

62
00:04:40,477 --> 00:04:44,375
The best decision on a windy day happens before you leave.

63
00:04:44,375 --> 00:04:47,131
This diagram shows an out and back route.

64
00:04:47,131 --> 00:04:53,112
Take the headwind leg first, while you are fresh, and come home with the wind behind you.

65
00:04:53,112 --> 00:04:59,497
It feels backwards, but the wind does not get easier later, and your legs definitely get worse.

66
00:04:59,497 --> 00:05:05,479
Being stranded far from home into a headwind on empty legs is how skaters end up walking.

67
00:05:05,479 --> 00:05:13,006
Pick loops with shelter, like tree lined roads or stretches behind a wall, and save your hard efforts for those.

68
00:05:13,006 --> 00:05:20,265
On very windy days, drop the long open stretches and use a smaller sheltered loop, even if it means repeats.

69
00:05:20,965 --> 00:05:23,142
This is where skaters lose money.

70
00:05:23,142 --> 00:05:26,704
Wind resistance is about your shape and your clothing.

71
00:05:26,704 --> 00:05:32,114
Bearings and wheels are a separate loss that matters less once the air takes over.

72
00:05:32,114 --> 00:05:36,600
A lower, flatter position is the biggest gain, and it costs nothing.

73
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:43,461
A close fitting race suit instead of a loose tee is a real gain, because loose fabric flaps like a sail.

74
00:05:43,461 --> 00:05:48,673
That is why a LiveLica Pro Skin Suit is worth wearing for a hard windy session.

75
00:05:48,673 --> 00:05:53,489
Clean, well oiled bearings help the whole ride but do not fight the wind.

76
00:05:53,489 --> 00:05:57,183
They simply let you know the air is what is slowing you.

77
00:05:57,183 --> 00:05:59,558
A bigger wheel does not help either.

78
00:05:59,558 --> 00:06:09,058
And gusty days are when you are most likely to get put down, so wear the gloves you keep skipping, and snug your helmet fit before you drop low.

79
00:06:09,758 --> 00:06:12,663
Average speed is the wrong measure in wind.

80
00:06:12,663 --> 00:06:19,419
Skate for the time you planned, at the effort you planned, and let the distance land where it lands.

81
00:06:19,419 --> 00:06:22,933
In intervals, hold the effort and let the pace drop.

82
00:06:22,933 --> 00:06:30,905
On easy days, hold an easy breathing rate even if that feels slow, because wind is how an easy day quietly turns hard.

83
00:06:30,905 --> 00:06:32,932
Here is a session worth doing.

84
00:06:32,932 --> 00:06:35,161
Warm up for fifteen minutes easy.

85
00:06:35,161 --> 00:06:44,147
Then do six repeats of two minutes hard on the exposed half, low and quick, ignoring speed, with the sheltered half easy as recovery.

86
00:06:44,147 --> 00:06:53,673
Finish with ten minutes easy and, if you still have legs, four sprints of about fifteen seconds with the wind behind you, thinking fast feet.

87
00:06:54,373 --> 00:06:56,509
Some days are not training days.

88
00:06:56,509 --> 00:07:10,125
Shorten the loop, move indoors or stay home when gusts shift you sideways between strokes, when you cannot hold a predictable line in traffic, or when debris, branches or dust are blowing across the road.

89
00:07:10,125 --> 00:07:16,599
Be stricter still on broken tarmac, because a gust on a bad patch is far worse than either alone.

90
00:07:16,599 --> 00:07:18,869
Protect your airways and eyes too.

91
00:07:18,869 --> 00:07:33,353
If a dusty windy session leaves you coughing, wheezy or with sore eyes for more than a day or two, or you get chest tightness or breathlessness that does not settle with rest, see a doctor before you train through it.

92
00:07:34,053 --> 00:07:36,745
If you remember one thing, make it this.

93
00:07:36,745 --> 00:07:44,550
Into the wind, get lower and narrower, shorten your stroke, and judge the day by effort and time, not average speed.

94
00:07:44,550 --> 00:07:48,655
Then head out into the wind and come home with it behind you.

95
00:07:48,655 --> 00:07:58,211
The full guide on the LiveLica blog includes a windy day checklist and answers to common questions about wheels, pack racing and arm position.

96
00:07:58,211 --> 00:08:00,297
The link is in the description.
